\section{Compare Results, Not Just Work}

\begin{verbatim}
oracle := full_positive_closure(edges)
slice  := saturate(magic_rules, edges, seed = main)
assert slice.answer == oracle.callees(main)  # same requested rows
assert util not in slice.need               # slice is narrower
edges := edges without (scan, parse)
repeat both evaluations on the new snapshot
assert slice.answer == {scan}               # old answer retracts
\end{verbatim}

The deletion test matters for an incremental front end. A completed ordinary table may outlive the input facts that justified it; use correctly declared incremental tabling or invalidate the relevant tables before rerunning after an edit. The rewritten Datalog program also needs a new fixed point for the new snapshot; \texttt{need(parse)} is no longer supported even though \texttt{parse} still has an outgoing edge. Within one immutable snapshot both rewritten operators are monotone in their accumulating relations. Snapshot deletion is a different problem.

There is a documented example of a source-to-rule boundary in Martin, Livshits, and Lam's PQL paper: its static approximation translates program queries into Datalog and uses bddbddb. The call-graph examples here illustrate tabling and magic sets independently; they are not claims about PQL's evaluation strategy. When a compiler issues thousands of similarly shaped queries, compare the number of reached demand keys and answer tuples against a shared full closure. If nearly every key is needed, guarding joins and storing many specialized tables can cost more than a well-indexed full analysis.

Cache a demand result under both its query key and the program snapshot. Its smaller output is valid only for that requested entry and version; it is not a replacement for a whole-program call-graph artifact.
